[[:db/retract 17592186307724 :answer-field/choices 17592186305316] [:db/retract 17592186308178 :answer-field/choices 17592186305585] {:db/id 17592186152279 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-10c6f8f6-9fca-5e23-8276-22a855906488"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-10c6f8f6-9fca-5e23-8276-22a855906488" :question/id #uuid "10c6f8f6-9fca-5e23-8276-22a855906488" :question/math-academy-id "q-334111" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=2{sin}^{2}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2{sin}^{2}⁡x & 1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {(2{sin}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} & {(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2{sin}^{2}⁡x & 1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2{sin}^{2}⁡x)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =4({sin}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-4({sin}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4dbf6a1b-898e-56e9-9f12-6a5e1d80b975" "answer-field-3672eb02-0003-53d9-90a8-1a9105690abc" "answer-field-a6385f95-6b30-5136-8980-da81fcd668ff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4dbf6a1b-898e-56e9-9f12-6a5e1d80b975" :answer-field/id #uuid "4dbf6a1b-898e-56e9-9f12-6a5e1d80b975" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-15e9135b-b871-5aba-b40d-ba3025b92b25" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2e5b64e6-9a77-5e45-9c0d-3ad872a7c207" "answer-15e9135b-b871-5aba-b40d-ba3025b92b25" "answer-273ff444-7dd9-5e5a-b414-0596922f638a" "answer-4e1519be-802b-5b17-9751-f17a087eadf1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e5b64e6-9a77-5e45-9c0d-3ad872a7c207" :answer/id #uuid "2e5b64e6-9a77-5e45-9c0d-3ad872a7c207" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15e9135b-b871-5aba-b40d-ba3025b92b25" :answer/id #uuid "15e9135b-b871-5aba-b40d-ba3025b92b25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-273ff444-7dd9-5e5a-b414-0596922f638a" :answer/id #uuid "273ff444-7dd9-5e5a-b414-0596922f638a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e1519be-802b-5b17-9751-f17a087eadf1" :answer/id #uuid "4e1519be-802b-5b17-9751-f17a087eadf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3672eb02-0003-53d9-90a8-1a9105690abc" :answer-field/id #uuid "3672eb02-0003-53d9-90a8-1a9105690abc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-daf94a0c-0e29-57f1-b831-528166c238c5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd836c36-70c9-53c0-b7fa-96d4837068ac" "answer-daf94a0c-0e29-57f1-b831-528166c238c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd836c36-70c9-53c0-b7fa-96d4837068ac" :answer/id #uuid "dd836c36-70c9-53c0-b7fa-96d4837068ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daf94a0c-0e29-57f1-b831-528166c238c5" :answer/id #uuid "daf94a0c-0e29-57f1-b831-528166c238c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a6385f95-6b30-5136-8980-da81fcd668ff" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6385f95-6b30-5136-8980-da81fcd668ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-1ab65ccb-a918-513f-ad62-ae4b2896ed85" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1ab65ccb-a918-513f-ad62-ae4b2896ed85" "answer-1e50b1fc-9bb0-519a-87b4-b41bfcf588bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ab65ccb-a918-513f-ad62-ae4b2896ed85" :answer/id #uuid "1ab65ccb-a918-513f-ad62-ae4b2896ed85" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "dependent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e50b1fc-9bb0-519a-87b4-b41bfcf588bd" :answer/id #uuid "1e50b1fc-9bb0-519a-87b4-b41bfcf588bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "independent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308561 :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x+1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{′} & ({x}^{2})^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(x+1)\\cdot (2x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2x(x+1)-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x \\\\  & =x(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307724 :answer-field/correct 17592186305356 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308178 :answer-field/correct 17592186307130 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8005871f-5013-58a1-b9ee-7ef831d491ac" "question-248767b0-4e8f-59a2-a18a-6d7a3f7d1129"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8005871f-5013-58a1-b9ee-7ef831d491ac" :question/id #uuid "8005871f-5013-58a1-b9ee-7ef831d491ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-334257" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-12y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nIII. The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{12}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{5}})+2x(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})-12(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x)+2x(3{x}^{2})-12({x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-750328c3-c578-5e21-8518-8f7c29a90ca6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-750328c3-c578-5e21-8518-8f7c29a90ca6" :answer-field/id #uuid "750328c3-c578-5e21-8518-8f7c29a90ca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7a565dca-df39-5e2d-b63f-4adbd2a8ea39" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e87d37de-a054-5273-bb9c-f325114b3de0" "answer-75105097-da69-5af8-857c-8da170024488" "answer-8a5318c9-c7a7-51b8-9b4d-6688604aeba7" "answer-7a565dca-df39-5e2d-b63f-4adbd2a8ea39" "answer-12840b9d-f4f7-5e1e-9319-49c92b0a1514"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e87d37de-a054-5273-bb9c-f325114b3de0" :answer/id #uuid "e87d37de-a054-5273-bb9c-f325114b3de0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75105097-da69-5af8-857c-8da170024488" :answer/id #uuid "75105097-da69-5af8-857c-8da170024488" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a5318c9-c7a7-51b8-9b4d-6688604aeba7" :answer/id #uuid "8a5318c9-c7a7-51b8-9b4d-6688604aeba7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a565dca-df39-5e2d-b63f-4adbd2a8ea39" :answer/id #uuid "7a565dca-df39-5e2d-b63f-4adbd2a8ea39" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12840b9d-f4f7-5e1e-9319-49c92b0a1514" :answer/id #uuid "12840b9d-f4f7-5e1e-9319-49c92b0a1514" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-248767b0-4e8f-59a2-a18a-6d7a3f7d1129" :question/id #uuid "248767b0-4e8f-59a2-a18a-6d7a3f7d1129" :question/math-academy-id "q-334262" :question/problem "$$\n4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+6x{y}^{′}-2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n\nIII. The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x).$ Notice that $\\frac{1}{x}={x}^{-1},$ so we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+6x(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-2(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{8}{x}-\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{2}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x).$ Notice that $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{\\frac{1}{2}},$ so we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2})+6x(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2})-2\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ (-{x}^{1/2}+3{x}^{1/2}-2{x}^{1/2}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-1} & {x}^{1/2} \\\\ -{x}^{-2} & \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}-{x}^{1/2}\\cdot (-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2}+{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4c538444-4b53-5d79-a4f2-bda9a48b5d41"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4c538444-4b53-5d79-a4f2-bda9a48b5d41" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c538444-4b53-5d79-a4f2-bda9a48b5d41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b3726556-58cf-54f7-a94e-a01800ba1dfe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-09ca4b98-0fe7-5c0e-bc3e-5d2f9d7b445d" "answer-da777daa-3338-5846-a305-378dd1e86eef" "answer-b3726556-58cf-54f7-a94e-a01800ba1dfe" "answer-61b68282-4cc9-5b93-be49-86af47b4a3b7" "answer-f3a6e302-ea43-50f3-855f-a828c356ceab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09ca4b98-0fe7-5c0e-bc3e-5d2f9d7b445d" :answer/id #uuid "09ca4b98-0fe7-5c0e-bc3e-5d2f9d7b445d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da777daa-3338-5846-a305-378dd1e86eef" :answer/id #uuid "da777daa-3338-5846-a305-378dd1e86eef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3726556-58cf-54f7-a94e-a01800ba1dfe" :answer/id #uuid "b3726556-58cf-54f7-a94e-a01800ba1dfe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61b68282-4cc9-5b93-be49-86af47b4a3b7" :answer/id #uuid "61b68282-4cc9-5b93-be49-86af47b4a3b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3a6e302-ea43-50f3-855f-a828c356ceab" :answer/id #uuid "f3a6e302-ea43-50f3-855f-a828c356ceab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-1a21c340-411e-558b-9b34-5988bd8860f9" :activity/id #uuid "1a21c340-411e-558b-9b34-5988bd8860f9" :activity/title "Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050150 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-a9c35b23-f5de-5890-a8e5-257e01ed501a" "step-feaa103c-ed4c-5dfc-900e-8d1325c89739" "step-5691033c-903f-5c2f-895d-3cf6dc88e9cf" "step-72926c4d-a224-5762-aec1-fec990efc5e6" "step-209bca2c-7d6d-5544-8ddd-af5776d68180"] :activity/first-step "step-a9c35b23-f5de-5890-a8e5-257e01ed501a" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a9c35b23-f5de-5890-a8e5-257e01ed501a" :step/id #uuid "a9c35b23-f5de-5890-a8e5-257e01ed501a" :step/content 17592186335741 :step/next "step-feaa103c-ed4c-5dfc-900e-8d1325c89739" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-feaa103c-ed4c-5dfc-900e-8d1325c89739" :step/id #uuid "feaa103c-ed4c-5dfc-900e-8d1325c89739" :step/content "question-10c6f8f6-9fca-5e23-8276-22a855906488" :step/next "step-5691033c-903f-5c2f-895d-3cf6dc88e9cf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5691033c-903f-5c2f-895d-3cf6dc88e9cf" :step/id #uuid "5691033c-903f-5c2f-895d-3cf6dc88e9cf" :step/content 17592186308561 :step/next "step-72926c4d-a224-5762-aec1-fec990efc5e6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-72926c4d-a224-5762-aec1-fec990efc5e6" :step/id #uuid "72926c4d-a224-5762-aec1-fec990efc5e6" :step/content "question-8005871f-5013-58a1-b9ee-7ef831d491ac" :step/next "step-209bca2c-7d6d-5544-8ddd-af5776d68180" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-209bca2c-7d6d-5544-8ddd-af5776d68180" :step/id #uuid "209bca2c-7d6d-5544-8ddd-af5776d68180" :step/content "question-248767b0-4e8f-59a2-a18a-6d7a3f7d1129" :db/ensure [:step/validate]}]
